</subsection>
<subsection xml:id="solutions">
    <title>Chapter 1.2</title>
    <ol>
        <li>
            <ol>
                <li>Conditional statement.</li>
                <li>Not a conditional statement (a question).</li>
                <li>Conditional statement.</li>
                <li>Conditional statement.</li>
                <li>Not a conditional statement (an order).</li>
                <li>Not a conditional statement (an order).</li>
            </ol>
        </li>

        <li>
            The proof is not valid because we did not assume <m>n</m> was even (the if-part of the initial statement). 
            Rather, we assumed <m>n</m> was one particular even number, so we only proved <m>2^3</m> is even. 
            Or, as a conditional statement, "if <m>n=2</m>, then <m>n^3</m> is even."
        </li>

        <li>
            <ol>
                <li>If I don't eat my hat, the Blue Jays did not win the World Series.</li>
                <li>If a shark is alive, it has not stopped moving.</li>
                <li>When the roads are not slippery, the weather is not cold.</li>
                <li>If <m>n</m> cannot be written as the sum of an even number and an odd number, then it is even.</li>
            </ol>
        </li>

        <li>
            <ol>
                <li>
                    False. 2 is prime and <m>2^2 = 4</m>, which is even. 
                    But if <m>p</m> is a prime number greater than 2, then the statement holds true.
                </li>
                <li></li>
            </ol>
        </li>
    </ol>
</subsection>


    </section>
